---

```{r setup, include = FALSE}
source("precompute-setup.R")
ehr_use_config(file.path(docs_root, "rendehr-demo/config/ehr_config.json"))
ehr_connect()
```

`ehr_connect()` stores the connection details; it does not test them until the
first query, so a bad login fails at the first read, not at connect time.

`merge_sources()` pools every source into one PostgreSQL schema. The schema
holds the cohort table, the admission-level flat table, the long time-series
table and the duration table. The `load_merged_*()` functions read them; each
takes the schema name you gave `merge_sources()` (here `"rendehr"`).

```{r cohort-info}
cohort <- load_merged_cohort_info("rendehr")
nrow(cohort)
length(unique(cohort$src_patient_id))
```

`cohort_only = TRUE` is the default: it returns the members of the cohort
definition. `FALSE` returns every imported row, including context that was
carried along for look-back but is not part of the cohort.

## One row per admission

The flat table is the analysis frame: one row per admission, with demographics,
admission characteristics, outcomes, and the first ICU stay's identifiers.

```{r flat}
flat <- load_merged_flat("rendehr")
dim(flat)
flat[1:4, c("src_patient_id", "age", "sex", "admission_type",
            "hospital_los", "hospital_mortality")]
```

The `ehr_id` column is the cross-source key. It identifies the same admission
in every merged table, so joins never depend on a source-specific ID.

## Long time series

The time-series table has one row per measurement: `concept_name`, time and
value, in a common unit per concept. `cols = "minimal"` keeps only the columns
needed for analysis and transfers less data.

```{r ts}
lab <- load_merged_ts(
  "rendehr", concepts = c("heart_rate", "lactate"),
  cols = "minimal"
)
dim(lab)
head(lab)
```

Time is carried twice: `time_since_hadm_min` measures from hospital admission,
`time_since_icu_min` from ICU admission. Both are in minutes, so the same table
supports admission-level and ICU-level analyses. `window_min = c(0, 1440)`
keeps the first 24 hours in SQL, on the admission clock or, with
`window_ref = "icu"`, the ICU clock.

One patient's stay, in the long format:

```{r patient}
pid <- flat$src_patient_id[1]
one <- get_patient_ts("mimic_iv_demo", pid, concepts = "heart_rate")
head(one[, c("concept_name", "time_since_icu_min", "value", "unit")])
```

`get_patient_*()` functions read the source schema directly, which is useful
when you need a single stay rather than a cohort-wide extract.
`get_patient_flat()` returns one row per admission.

## Wide by time bin

`load_merged_ts_wide()` pivots in SQL: name the concepts and the aggregates you
want, choose a bin width in minutes, and optionally a window in bin units. With
hourly bins, `window = c(0, 23)` keeps the first 24 hours.

```{r wide}
day1 <- load_merged_ts_wide(
  "rendehr",
  spec = list(heart_rate = c("mean", "max"), lactate = "max", sbp = "min"),
  bin_min = 60, window = c(0, 23)
)
dim(day1)
head(day1[, c("ehr_id", "t_bin", "heart_rate_mean", "heart_rate_max", "lactate")])
```

One aggregate per concept names the column after the concept (`lactate`); two
or more add the aggregate (`heart_rate_mean`, `heart_rate_max`).

`complete_grid = TRUE` emits every bin in the window, with `NA` where a concept
was not measured. It regularises the shape only; it does not decide who is
eligible for the analysis.

`clean = "range"` drops readings outside each concept's plausible range in the
concept dictionary before aggregating; `ranges` overrides the bounds. Each
concept gains a `<concept>_n_cleaned` column with the readings dropped in that
bin, and the `clean_report` attribute gives the totals and bounds per concept:

```{r wide-clean}
day1_clean <- load_merged_ts_wide(
  "rendehr", spec = list(heart_rate = "max", lactate = "max"),
  bin_min = 60, window = c(0, 23), clean = "range"
)
attr(day1_clean, "clean_report")
```

Plotting the cohort's heart rate over the first day is then a join and a line:

```{r trajectory, fig.alt = "Median hourly heart rate in the first 24 hours of admission, split by in-hospital death, in the MIMIC-IV demo Sepsis-3 cohort."}
library(ggplot2)
d <- merge(day1, flat[, c("ehr_id", "hospital_mortality")], by = "ehr_id")
agg <- aggregate(heart_rate_mean ~ t_bin + hospital_mortality, data = d,
                 FUN = median, na.rm = TRUE)
ggplot(agg, aes(t_bin, heart_rate_mean,
                colour = factor(hospital_mortality,
                                labels = c("Survived", "Died")))) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1) +
  labs(x = "Hours since admission", y = "Median heart rate (bpm)",
       colour = NULL) +
  theme_minimal()
```

## Durations

The duration table has one row per interval concept: drug exposure, ventilation,
and other state that lasts. `duration_min` is the length in minutes.

```{r dur}
dur <- load_merged_dur("rendehr")
dim(dur)
head(dur[, c("ehr_id", "concept_name", "duration_min")])
```

## What is in the schema

`list_concepts()` reports the concepts available for a source and their layer:
the package dictionary, adapted derived concepts, your own SQL, and external
modules. `concept_summary()` gives counts and value summaries for one concept.

```{r concepts}
concepts <- list_concepts("mimic_iv_demo")
table(concepts$schema)
concepts[concepts$concept_name %in% c("lactate", "creatinine", "sofa"),
         c("concept_name", "schema", "origin_name")]
concept_summary("mimic_iv_demo", "lactate")
```

